 "location": {
  "name": "Gal\u00e1pagos Islands",
  "wiki": "Gal\u00e1pagos_Islands",
  "country": "Ecuador",
  "region": "Gal\u00e1pagos Province, eastern Pacific Ocean",
  "lat": -0.5,
  "lon": -90.5,
  "tagline": "Volcanic islands on the equator where the wildlife still doesn't run away.",
  "intro": "The Gal\u00e1pagos are a chain of volcanic islands straddling the equator roughly 1,000 km west of mainland Ecuador. Isolation produced animals found nowhere else \u2014 giant tortoises, sea-going iguanas, tropical penguins \u2014 and their variations helped shape Charles Darwin's thinking on natural selection after his 1835 visit. Nearly all of the land is national park, and the islands were among the first places named a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1978"
   },
   {
    "label": "Islands, islets & rocks",
    "value": "127"
   },
   {
    "label": "Land area",
    "value": "\u22488,000 km\u00b2"
   },
   {
    "label": "Highest point",
    "value": "Volc\u00e1n Wolf, 1,707 m"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "The islands are named for their giant tortoises: 'gal\u00e1pago' is an old Spanish word for tortoise, and the name already appears on Ortelius's map published in 1570.",
   "The marine iguana is the only iguana in the world that feeds in the sea \u2014 and during the 1982\u201383 El Ni\u00f1o, about 70% of them starved when their algae died back.",
